WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.119
You wrote a query.

00:00:04.119 --> 00:00:06.239
The syntax is fine.

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.358
The result looks wrong.

00:00:08.358 --> 00:00:10.477
Or worse, it looks plausible but is wrong.

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:12.597
How do you check?

00:00:12.597 --> 00:00:14.716
The slow, careful, reliable way: write a debug table.

00:00:14.716 --> 00:00:16.836
List every input row.

00:00:16.836 --> 00:00:18.955
Walk the query clause by clause.

00:00:18.955 --> 00:00:21.074
Cross out the rows that drop.

00:00:21.074 --> 00:00:23.194
The survivors are the real answer.

00:00:23.194 --> 00:00:25.313
People also call this a hand-trace.

00:00:25.313 --> 00:00:30.275
Why bother, when the query already runs?

00:00:30.275 --> 00:00:35.238
Three reasons.

00:00:35.238 --> 00:00:40.200
First, LLMs are great at syntax but shaky on semantics, sixteen to eighty-two percent on the BIRD benchmark, so a query can parse cleanly and still be logically wrong.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.163
Second, the classic interview question is exactly this: is this query correct?

00:00:45.163 --> 00:00:50.125
Reading the logic is what gets you hired.

00:00:50.125 --> 00:00:55.088
Third, it is simply faster.

00:00:55.088 --> 00:01:00.050
Re-prompting an LLM can burn thirty minutes; drawing one debug table takes five, and you build real intuition.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:03.613
Here is a query that claims it returns four user-genre pairs.

00:01:03.613 --> 00:01:07.176
Let's hand-trace it, side by side, and check.

00:01:07.176 --> 00:01:10.740
Step one: FROM and JOIN.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.303
Nine listens, each tagged with its genre.

00:01:14.303 --> 00:01:17.866
That is the working set.

00:01:17.866 --> 00:01:21.429
Step two: GROUP BY user and genre.

00:01:21.429 --> 00:01:24.992
The nine rows collapse into six buckets, one per user-genre pair, each carrying its count.

00:01:24.992 --> 00:01:28.555
Step three: HAVING count greater than or equal to two.

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:32.119
Only three buckets survive: Mickey-Pop, Minnie-Classic, Daffy-Pop.

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:35.682
Three pairs, not four.

00:01:35.682 --> 00:01:39.245
The trace shows exactly which rows dropped out.

00:01:39.245 --> 00:01:43.262
For a table with millions of rows, you obviously cannot list every one.

00:01:43.262 --> 00:01:47.279
You don't.

00:01:47.279 --> 00:01:51.296
You trace a representative handful, ten or twenty rows that hit every interesting case: a NULL, an edge value, a boundary.

00:01:51.296 --> 00:01:55.313
And you approximate the rest with shorthand.

00:01:55.313 --> 00:01:59.330
First few, middle ties, last two.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:03.347
If the query handles the sample, it almost certainly handles the whole table.

00:02:03.347 --> 00:02:07.613
Before you trust any query, run down five things.

00:02:07.613 --> 00:02:11.879
JOINs: is the row count what you expect, and is it the right kind of join?

00:02:11.879 --> 00:02:16.145
NULLs: handled correctly, IS NULL not equals NULL?

00:02:16.145 --> 00:02:20.410
Groups: the right granularity, user or user-and-genre?

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.676
Aggregates: COUNT star or COUNT of a column?

00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:28.942
And windows: the right partition, and RANK versus ROW_NUMBER on ties?

00:02:28.942 --> 00:02:33.208
Each one is a place real queries quietly go wrong.

00:02:33.208 --> 00:02:37.155
The hand-trace is the most underused tool in SQL.

00:02:37.155 --> 00:02:41.103
It costs five minutes.

00:02:41.103 --> 00:02:45.050
It catches bugs that would otherwise survive into production.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.998
And in an interview, it is what separates someone who knows SQL from someone who reads a query and crosses their fingers.
